<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 365 0 R
/T (obvious, slow-motion foul on live TV. Instead of accepting the clear video evidence, the fan insists the referee rigged the game against their team. Conformity might also mean outsourcing your thinking to the group itself. For instance, a person might believe that birds are evil, not because they have evidence for it, but simply because they conform to a group which collectively agrees that it is the case that birds are evil.)
/E (obvious, slow-motion foul on live TV. Instead of accepting the clear video evidence, the fan insists the referee rigged the game against their team. Conformity might also mean outsourcing your thinking to the group itself. For instance, a person might believe that birds are evil, not because they have evidence for it, but simply because they conform to a group which collectively agrees that it is the case that birds are evil.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 365 0 R
/E ( Ultimately Bonhoeffer argues that stupidity is more dangerous than evil because it has a greater potential to damage our lives. A powerful idiot can cause more harm than a powerful robber. This is also made evident by the fact that evil is generally far more visible and less tolerated than stupidity is. We know when there is evil and we can deny its power. For example, we can identify a robber when we catch them stealing and the justice system can put the criminal in prison. But in the case of stupidity, not only is it not always clearly )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
