/E (Elijah possesses a strong moral framework, but his morality is context-dependent. He can be compassionate, generous and protective while simultaneously committing acts that would be horrifying by ordinary ethical standards. This is not necessarily evidence of hypocrisy. Rather, Elijah's moral system operates through competing values: protection of family, preservation of life, loyalty, justice, personal honour and individual compassion.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
620 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 372 0 R
/E (When these values conflict, family loyalty usually wins. This is where Elijah demonstrates something resembling compartmentalization.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
621 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 372 0 R
/E (Compartmentalization is a psychological defence mechanism through which conflicting thoughts, emotions or behaviours are separated into distinct mental compartments so that they can coexist without overwhelming the individual. Elijah can maintain an image of himself as a gentleman and protector while accepting the necessity of violence. He does not have to believe that he is a monster in order to commit monstrous acts; he can categorise those acts as necessary actions performed in defence of something morally meaningful.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
622 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 372 0 R
/E (And yet the fact that he experiences guilt, regret and compassion suggests that his conscience has not disappeared. Quite the opposite. His psychological conflict is partly produced because his conscience remains extremely active. Elijah is therefore not a character without morality. He is a character whose morality is constantly at war with his loyalty.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [6]
>>
