/P 377 0 R
/E (refusing to abandon him. He could recognise Klaus's darkness without believing that the darkness was all he was. He could condemn his brother's actions and still love the person underneath them. He could spend centuries trying to save Klaus from himself, even when Klaus repeatedly made that task almost comically impossible. And when the end finally came, Elijah was faced with the one thing he had spent his entire life trying to prevent: a world in which Klaus would have to face eternity without his brother beside him.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
675 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (So Elijah made the choice that, psychologically, was both devastating and completely consistent. He chose Klaus. He chose to die with him. There is something almost unbearably poignant about the fact that Elijah, who had spent centuries believing his purpose was to protect Klaus, ultimately could not conceive of protection as surviving him. His final act was not another attempt to control Klaus's fate, redeem him or persuade him to become someone else. It was simply companionship. For once, Elijah did not try to save his brother from death. He decided that Klaus would not have to meet it alone. The protector finally stopped protecting; the mediator stopped negotiating; the brother stopped trying to repair what could no longer be repaired. He simply stayed.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
676 0 obj
<<
