/T (       When we hear the word cult, we often picture mysterious rituals, isolated compounds, or people who blindly follow a leader or consider them a god. It feels like something that belongs in documentaries rather than in our real lives. Psychology reveals a far more scary truth: cult members are rarely foolish or unintelligent. Many are students, parents, artists and people who were simply searching for belonging, hope or purpose to live by. The psychology of cults is not about strange people making strange choices. It is about understanding how regular human needs until loyalty is replaced with their own freedom. )
/E (       When we hear the word cult, we often picture mysterious rituals, isolated compounds, or people who blindly follow a leader or consider them a god. It feels like something that belongs in documentaries rather than in our real lives. Psychology reveals a far more scary truth: cult members are rarely foolish or unintelligent. Many are students, parents, artists and people who were simply searching for belonging, hope or purpose to live by. The psychology of cults is not about strange people making strange choices. It is about understanding how regular human needs until loyalty is replaced with their own freedom. )
/Pg 36 0 R
