722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (People usually join because they are attracted to hope, not control. There are quite a few methods and stages of reeling a person into a cult, leaving off their family, friends or even their principles. Love Bombing is the illusion of unconditional love and acceptance. It is one of the most common recruitment techniques. New members receive overwhelming kindness. They are praised, hugged, invited to gatherings and made to feel valued almost immediately. At first, the affection feels genuine. The psychological effect is powerful because our brains naturally respond positively to acceptance and social approval. Feeling appreciated releases positive emotions that strengthen trust and attachment. However, in many cults this affection gradually becomes conditional. Approval depends on obedience. Questioning anything begins to threaten acceptance. They train your mind suchthat you'll be loved only if you cut off your family, perform a specific ritual or surrender your assets \( materialistic or physical\). The Power of Charismatic Leaders)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 383 0 R
/E (Nearly every famous cult has been built around a charismatic leader. Sociologist Max Weber described charismatic authority as power that comes from followers believing someone possesses extraordinary qualities rather than official qualifications.These leaders often claim to have special spiritual knowledge, unique solutions to society's problems or an important destiny that others cannot understand.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
