892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 400 0 R
/E (impression in one area influences how we perceive other characteristics. They're funny, so we assume they're kind. They're confident, so we assume they're intelligent. They remember one tiny detail about us, so suddenly we think they're incredibly thoughtful. A single positive quality can begin colouring the way we interpret everything else about them, sometimes before we have enough information to know whether those assumptions are actually true. Sometimes, we aren't simply discovering who someone is. We're filling in the blanks. This is why attraction can involve a certain amount of idealisation. We may see someone's best moments, imagine their intentions, remember the moments that made us feel good, and unconsciously construct a version of them that is partly based on reality and partly based on our own expectations. When we like someone, our perception doesn't necessarily become dishonest; it simply becomes selective \(standards\). We naturally pay more attention to the things that support the emotional story already forming in our minds.)
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
