896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 400 0 R
/E (When someone becomes emotionally significant, the brain can begin giving them an unusual amount of attention, which means even ordinary things can suddenly remind you of them. A song, a place, a particular time of day, a phrase, a notification sound or something as simple as seeing their favourite food can bring them back into your thoughts. And uncertainty can make this even stronger. If you already know someone likes you, there may be less mystery, but if you're constantly wondering, Did they mean something by that? Why did they reply like that? Do they actually like me? Why haven't they texted?, your brain keeps returning to the unanswered question. Uncertainty gives the mind something to solve and humans are remarkably drawn to unfinished stories. When there is no clear answer, the brain can continue revisiting the same situation, searching for clues and trying to predict what might happen next. This isn't limited to romantic relationships either. We can experience a similar mental pull when waiting for an exam result, wondering whether we got selected for something, waiting for an important message or worrying about how someone interpreted something we said. The less certain we are about an emotionally important outcome, the more our attention may keep circling back to it. )
/Pg 54 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
