931 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 405 0 R
/E (What do we see when we look at someone struggling with a mental illness or addiction? A patient, an addict, a person with schizophrenia, someone who hurts themselves or simply someone who needs to be treated? These are words we often use because they help us describe what is happening, but during our visit to a rehabilitation centre, I began to wonder whether, somewhere between the diagnosis and the treatment, we sometimes forget the person. Behind every diagnosis is a life, behind every addiction is a history and behind every symptom is an experience that we may never completely understand. Behind every person sitting in a rehabilitation centre is someone who once had dreams, relationships, fears, ambitions, memories and an identity that existed long before they were given a label. The visit made mental health feel very different from the way it appears in a textbook. Psychology is often introduced through definitions, symptoms, causes and treatments, where disorders become categories and behaviours become symptoms that can be identified and classified. But meeting people who have actually lived through these experiences makes one thing impossible to ignore: a human being can never be reduced to a diagnosis. A diagnosis may tell us something about what a person is experiencing, but it can never tell us everything about who that person is, what they have been through, what they have lost, what they hope for or what they may still become.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
