/E ( Their experience of the world can become fundamentally different from ours. Two people can stand in the same room and experience completely different realities: for us, there may be silence, while for someone experiencing an auditory hallucination, there may be a voice; for us, there may be an ordinary movement of the fingers, while for that person, the movement may carry an entirely different meaning. Understanding this does not mean accepting every belief as fact. It means recognising the difference between disagreeing with someone's perception and dismissing the person experiencing it. )
/Pg 62 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
