/P 425 0 R
/T (Why We Become Obsessed with People We Have Never Met)
/E (Why We Become Obsessed with People We Have Never Met)
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1095 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 425 0 R
/E (The psychology becomes even more fascinating when we move from teams to individual athletes. Why does someone we have never met become emotionally important to us? Why can a stranger's retirement produce genuine sadness? Part of the explanation lies in parasocial relationships, a concept used to describe the one-sided sense of familiarity or intimacy that audiences can develop with public figures. Athletes today are no longer visible only during matches. Through interviews, documentaries, Instagram posts, training videos and behind-the-scenes content, they appear repeatedly in the everyday lives of their audiences. Repeated exposure creates familiarity and familiarity can create attachment. Social media has intensified this process because it creates the impression that the athlete is not merely appearing before us but allowing us access to their personal world.)
/Pg 79 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
