/E (Messi's international career is now over. Ronaldo's era, too, is approaching its twilight. The stadiums will remain, the clubs will continue, the competitions will return and another group of young players will eventually begin occupying the spaces once reserved for the legends we thought could never be replaced. A child who has never seen Messi play live will one day describe him as we describe players from an earlier age: through old footage, photographs and stories told by people who were there. That is how sport survives. Not through permanence, but through inheritance.One generation watches a player; the next generation watches the highlights; the generation after that hears the stories. The statistics become records, the records become history and history becomes nostalgia. Perhaps that is why sport has endured for so long. We do not love it merely because we love winning. We love it because it gives us something increasingly difficult to find in ordinary life: a moment in which millions of people can feel the same thing at the same time. For ninety minutes, strangers become a community; a player becomes a hero; a stadium becomes a memory; and a game becomes a story that may outlive everyone who watched it.)
/Pg 85 0 R
