1183 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 436 0 R
/E (This doesn't necessarily mean our brains are deliberately lying to us. Memory is selective for a reason. We tend to pay greater attention to emotionally significant experiences and positive memories can become particularly comforting when we're stressed, lonely or uncertain about the present. When life feels overwhelming, the brain may naturally search its memory for experiences associated with safety, familiarity, connection or happiness. That is one reason nostalgia can be emotionally useful. Remembering a time when you felt loved or belonged can temporarily remind you that you have experienced those feelings before. The past becomes a psychological comfort blanket.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
